# Everything text is LF, in the working tree as well as in the repository.
#
# This is not a preference, it is what the repository already contains - every tracked source
# file is LF - and stating it here is what keeps it that way. Without it the rule lived only in
# .editorconfig, where it was set to crlf by mistake: editors then rewrote whole files on the
# first keystroke and buried real changes under thousands of line-ending-only diffs. Git now
# normalises on commit regardless of which editor, OS, or tool did the writing.
*.cs      text eol=lf
*.axaml   text eol=lf
*.xaml    text eol=lf
*.csproj  text eol=lf
*.sln     text eol=lf
*.props   text eol=lf
*.targets text eol=lf
*.json    text eol=lf
*.md      text eol=lf
*.yml     text eol=lf
*.yaml    text eol=lf
*.xml     text eol=lf
*.txt     text eol=lf
*.editorconfig text eol=lf

# Binary fixtures and assets git must never touch. The iPod database fixtures and the CDB
# skeleton are byte-exact oracles - a single translated byte invalidates a conformance test
# and the failure reads as a parser bug.
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.ico     binary
*.ithmb   binary
*.cdb     binary
*.iTunesDB binary
*.nupkg   binary
*.zip     binary

# Shell scripts MUST keep LF endings in the working tree, on every OS.
#
# Without this, git's autocrlf hands a Windows checkout CRLF-terminated .sh files, and
# copying one to a Mac or Linux box produces failures that read as anything but a line
# ending: `set -euo pipefail` becomes `pipefail\r`, and bash reports "invalid option name"
# - which sends you looking at the shell version instead of the file. That cost a build
# cycle on the macOS ffmpeg script.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the other things a non-Windows host executes or parses strictly.
*.bash text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf
*.desktop text eol=lf
